  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe will combine multiple optimization techniques to minimize the evaluation function J(a) = 2*n*max(conv(a,a)) / (sum(a))^2 for a sequence of length 768. The algorithm alternates between gradient-based optimization (ADMM) and local search using coordinate descent. The key idea is to iteratively refine the sequence by focusing on the indices that contribute most to the maximum autocorrelation value, ensuring that each step maintains non-negativity while improving J.\n\nMathematical Specification\nGiven a sequence a (non-negative) of length 768, let:\nn = 768\ns = sum(a) (defending against too small or NaN values by projection)\nb = FFT-based convolution of a with itself (full mode)\nM = max(b) (maximum autocorrelation value)\n\nThe objective is to minimize J = (2*n*M) / (s^2) subject to a[i] \u2265 0.\n\nWe represent the optimization variable x\u2080 as the weight vector to normalize, and use the method of multipliers (ADMM) to transform the problem into a coordinate-wise parameter update.\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Generate multiple initial sequences of length 768:\n   - Uniform initialization: a\u2080 = np.ones(768)/768, normalized to sum=1\n   - Gaussian-like with cutoff at 0.01: normalize sum of exp(-distance^2) to 1\n   - Cosine windows for frequencies 0.25, 0.5, 0.75: normalized to sum=1\n   - Parabolic initialization (concave): (1-t)^2 + 0.05 (convex): (1-t)^2 * (-1 + 2*float(frequency))\n2. Select the best initial sequence (lowest J) for further optimization.\n3. Set up constraints for the ADMM iteration:\n   - First constraint: non-negativity a[i] \u2265 0\n   - Second constraint: clamping to avoid values that would make J unstable (a[i] not too large)\n4. Execute the two-phase ADMM optimization:\n   Phase 1: Projected gradient with clamping (for large steps) to approximate the solution.\n   Phase 2: Fine-tune using coordinate descent to reduce M significantly while keeping s stable.\n5. After each phase, use the evaluate_sequence function to track progress and find % cheaper solutions.\n6. Continue alternating between phases 1 and 2 until no improvement or deadlock.\n\nSearch Choices and Useful Variations\n1. Initializations will be varied to explore different potential minimum structures.\n2. Clamping bounds are set dynamically based on the current J value.\n3. Parameter \u03b7 for the multiplier method is annealed from 0.3 to 0.05.\n4. Temperature tau for covariance computation ranges between [0.1, 0.5] for stability.\n5. Time allocation: Reserve 400s for ADMM and 500s for coordinate descent across multiple restarts.\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness\nTotal time: 1500s\n- Start each phase with a limited time budget and % of available CPU.\n- Add termination conditions:\n   - If J doesn't improve for % of iterations.\n   - If new solution exceeds max_coeff (set to 100) due to potential overflow.\n   - If Task-based constraints are violated, re-run fallback.\n   - If solution becomes all zeros/negative, restart with cached states.\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n1. Fallback: If evaluate_sequence fails, revert to x\u2080 or the best candidate from previous phase.\n2. Defensive programming for sequence evaluations:\n   - Use numba for just-in-time compilation of the evaluation and optimization functions.\n   - Lock hyperparameters to avoid memory bloat (max_coeff, clamp factor).\n3. Format final solution: Explicitly output coefficients [0.0 to 100] for the exact length 768, passing to evaluate_sequence.",
